Problem

Today, grocery items carry a relative tag weekScope: "this" | "next". When the calendar week rolls over, items previously tagged "next" still appear under the Next week tab instead of moving into This week. Eggs and Greek yogurt added last week as "Next week" are stuck there.

Solution

Replace the relative tag with an absolute ISO week anchor on each grocery item. Tabs derive their content from the current ISO week at render time.

1. Data model (stored in grocery_lists.items JSON — no migration needed)

Add a new field to each item:

interface GroceryItem {
  // …existing fields
  isoWeek?: string;          // e.g. "2026-W25"  (ISO year + ISO week)
  weekScope?: "this" | "next"; // kept for back-compat read; no longer written
}

Helpers in src/lib/groceryUtils.ts:





isoWeekKey(date: Date): string → "2026-W25"



currentWeekKey(), nextWeekKey()



compareWeekKeys(a, b) for ordering

2. Write path (useGroceryList.ts)

When addItemWithMeta / bulkAddItems* is called with weekScope: "this" | "next", stamp:





"this" → isoWeek = currentWeekKey()



"next" → isoWeek = nextWeekKey()

We keep the weekScope parameter on the public API for callers (MaSemaine, AddToListSheet, GroceryList) — they don't need to know about ISO keys. Internally we resolve it to an absolute isoWeek at write time. Dedup sameScope check uses isoWeek equality.

3. Read path (GroceryList.tsx, SharedList.tsx)

const thisKey = currentWeekKey();
const nextKey = nextWeekKey();

bucketFor(item):
  key = item.isoWeek ?? legacyResolve(item)
  if key <= thisKey  → "this"   // overdue items roll forward automatically
  if key === nextKey → "next"
  if key  > nextKey  → "next"   // far-future items shown in Next (rare)

legacyResolve (one-time backfill on read for items missing isoWeek):





weekScope === "next" → nextKey of the list's updated_at at the time it was written; since we don't have a per-item timestamp, fall back to currentWeekKey() so old "next" items roll into This week as the user expects.



otherwise → currentWeekKey().

After the first mutation, items get a real isoWeek written, so legacy ambiguity is short-lived.

